# Fluid Typography & Spacing System

A fluid typography and spacing system scales text sizes and layout spacing smoothly within defined limits as the available viewport or container size changes. It uses a coordinated set of rules rather than unrelated values for each screen width.

Fluid values can coexist with breakpoints. A layout may change its structure at a breakpoint while selected sizes adjust continuously between minimum and maximum values.

## A CSS example

CSS clamp() accepts a minimum, a preferred value, and a maximum:

```css
:root {
  --text-heading: clamp(2rem, calc(1rem + 3vw), 4rem);
  --space-section: clamp(0.75rem, calc(0.5rem + 1vw), 1.5rem);
}

h1 {
  font-size: var(--text-heading);
}

section {
  padding-block: var(--space-section);
}
```

Here, the viewport contribution changes with screen width, while the rem values relate to the root font size. These values are an illustration to test in context, not a universal scale.

## What to test

Inspect intermediate widths as well as the endpoints. Check long headings, translated labels, narrow controls, and realistic content.

Use relative units thoughtfully and test browser zoom and user font settings. Combining rem with a viewport unit does not automatically ensure accessible text resizing.

WCAG 2.2's Resize Text criterion generally requires text to resize to 200% without loss of content or functionality, subject to its exceptions. Reflow is a separate requirement with defined viewport conditions and exceptions for content needing a two-dimensional layout.

Keep line length, line height, focus indicators, and target sizes usable as the layout changes. Avoid allowing a fluid scale to make essential text or controls too small.

